## Proposed solution

I'm fairly desperate (this will hold up the EU-DEMO build soon), so I'd be open to a quick fix (probably adding in some more layers of `check_reversed`, etc.) but I do fear this will just come back to haunt us again in the future.

So I'd propose that we just cache the `_shape` of a `BluemiraGeo` rather than reconstructing them each time from wires.

## Alternative solutions

Perhaps there are other ways of doing this? What I think would be good to have:

* less code reverse engineering FreeCAD's reconstruction logic of wires -> faces -> shells -> solids
* more robust geometry operations (we have come across a few cases of operations succeeding in OCCT but failing when we reconstruct)
* preservation of the potential to label geometries (even if it is a posteriori)

## Additional context

As mentioned this is holding up the EU-DEMO build as I can't pattern the VV. I can proceed without for now but it's a bit of shame. Entirely possible we see the same issue with the VVTS which is coming up soon too

## Description of issue / requirement to address

At present, in `bluemira/equilibria/fem_fixed_boundary/fem_magnetostatic_2D.py::FemGradShafranovFixedBoundary` we do the following to get the magnetic flux on axis and at the boundary:

```python
    @property
    def psi_ax(self):
        """Poloidal flux on the magnetic axis"""
        return np.max(self.psi.vector()[:])

    @property
    def psi_b(self):
        """Poloidal flux on the boundary"""
        return np.min(self.psi.vector()[:])
```

Right now, we return the maximum from the mesh points, but for a coarse mesh this might be leading to oscillations of the magnetic axis (e.g. forwards-backwards-forwards) across iterations. This could be slowing down convergence.

## Proposed solution

I reckon we can improve the convergence of the fixed boundary G-S solve by using optimisation to find the psi on axis more accurately. 

Much like we now do for finding the plasma shape parameters of the 95th flux surface, we could also use optimisation to find the maximum magnetic flux value in the function space.

I also recommend we cache the two above properties, to avoid repetition of calculations. This is not a bottleneck at all, but if we add in optimisation it will slow things down if called every time psi_norm is calculated.

## Alternative solutions
It does work as is!

## Additional context
Splitting this out as a separate issue because #1067 has been a long time coming and I don't want to slow it down any more than I already have. Plus the functionality therein already works fine. I will pick this up once the FEM stuff goes in.

